1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,363
This video solves the fall 2019 midterm of E C E 320, all four questions, in order.

2
00:00:08,813 --> 00:00:13,314
Question 1, 10 marks: performance, hazards and Amdahl's law.

3
00:00:13,764 --> 00:00:22,127
Question 2, 22 marks: a pipeline diagram, a forwarding table, a stall table, and a forwarding optimization.

4
00:00:22,577 --> 00:00:29,553
Question 3, 12 marks: cache address bits, addresses in the same set, and an L R U pattern.

5
00:00:30,003 --> 00:00:36,915
Question 4, 12 marks: a pipeline design question, with new registers HI and LO.

6
00:00:37,365 --> 00:00:45,130
The paper gives 60 minutes. Its instructions say: if information is missing, state an assumption and continue.

7
00:00:45,580 --> 00:00:55,543
Question 1 part A, 4 marks. Half of program P can be accelerated by a factor of 4. Find the speedup. Pause the video and try it.

8
00:00:55,993 --> 00:01:02,670
50 percent of the program can be accelerated. So the accelerated fraction f is point 5.

9
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:08,368
The accelerated part becomes 4 times faster. So its speedup s is 4.

10
00:01:08,818 --> 00:01:16,839
Draw the old time as one bar of length 1. The gray half is not accelerated. The blue half is accelerated.

11
00:01:17,289 --> 00:01:20,297
In the new time, the gray half stays point 5.

12
00:01:20,747 --> 00:01:27,638
The blue half becomes 4 times shorter. Point 5 divided by 4 is point 1 2 5.

13
00:01:28,088 --> 00:01:34,061
So the new time is point 5 plus point 1 2 5. That is point 6 2 5.

14
00:01:34,511 --> 00:01:44,965
This is Amdahl's law. The speedup is the old time divided by the new time. That is 1 divided by the sum of 1 minus f and f over s.

15
00:01:45,415 --> 00:01:54,076
Put in the values: 1 divided by point 5 plus point 1 2 5. That is 1 divided by point 6 2 5.

16
00:01:54,526 --> 00:01:57,129
The answer: the speedup is 1.6.

17
00:01:57,579 --> 00:02:13,707
Question 1 part B, 4 marks. Find all RAW, WAW and WAR pairs in these four instructions. Write each pair as a b, where a and b are instruction numbers. Pause the video and try it.

18
00:02:14,157 --> 00:02:23,117
First, list the reads and writes of each instruction. Instruction 1, the add, reads x1 and x3, and writes x1.

19
00:02:23,567 --> 00:02:27,812
Instruction 2, the load word, reads x2 and writes x1.

20
00:02:28,262 --> 00:02:34,086
Instruction 3, the store word, reads x1 and x3. It writes no register.

21
00:02:34,536 --> 00:02:39,805
Instruction 4, the subtract, reads x2 and x1, and writes x4.

22
00:02:40,255 --> 00:02:45,205
Now the RAW pairs. Instructions 3 and 4 read x1.

23
00:02:45,655 --> 00:02:53,847
Their most recent older writer of x1 is instruction 2. So the RAW pairs are 2 3 and 2 4.

24
00:02:54,297 --> 00:03:02,382
The pairs 1 3 and 1 4 are not RAW pairs, because instruction 2 writes x1 in between.

25
00:03:02,832 --> 00:03:11,451
Now the WAW pairs. Instructions 1 and 2 both write x1. So 1 2 is a WAW pair.

26
00:03:11,901 --> 00:03:22,738
Now the WAR pairs. Instruction 1 reads x1, and then instruction 2 writes x1. So 1 2 is also a WAR pair.

27
00:03:23,188 --> 00:03:27,860
The answer: RAW 2 3 and 2 4, WAW 1 2, and WAR 1 2.

28
00:03:28,310 --> 00:03:35,819
Question 1 part C, 2 marks. Why do micro-architects use Amdahl's law? Pause the video and try it.

29
00:03:36,269 --> 00:03:45,741
Use the numbers of part A. f is point 5. With s equal to 4, the new time is point 6 2 5. The speedup is 1.6.

30
00:03:46,191 --> 00:03:56,239
Make the blue part 10 times faster. It becomes point 0 5. The new time is point 5 5, and the speedup is only 1.82.

31
00:03:56,689 --> 00:04:04,113
Make the blue part infinitely fast. It becomes 0. The new time is point 5, and the speedup is 2.

32
00:04:04,563 --> 00:04:12,798
The gray part never becomes shorter. So the speedup can never be more than 1 divided by 1 minus f. Here that is 2.

33
00:04:13,248 --> 00:04:29,547
A good answer: Amdahl's law gives the overall speedup of a change before it is built. The part that the change does not improve limits the gain. So architects make the common case fast, and do not spend hardware on rare cases.

34
00:04:29,997 --> 00:04:41,005
Question 1, the answers to write. Part A: the speedup is 1 divided by point 6 2 5, which is 1.6. Show the formula and the values.

35
00:04:41,455 --> 00:04:48,729
Part B: RAW 2 3 and 2 4. WAW 1 2. WAR 1 2. The four instructions are on the right.

36
00:04:49,179 --> 00:05:00,422
Part C: Amdahl's law gives the overall speedup before you build the change. The part that is not improved limits the gain. So make the common case fast.

37
00:05:00,872 --> 00:05:06,781
Question 2, pipelined execution, 22 marks. It has 14 instructions.

38
00:05:07,231 --> 00:05:12,693
The pipeline has forwarding as in class, but no forwarding into the decode stage.

39
00:05:13,143 --> 00:05:20,481
Branch direction and target are known in X. The pipeline guesses not taken until the branch resolves.

40
00:05:20,931 --> 00:05:23,555
The branch in instruction 8 is taken.

41
00:05:24,005 --> 00:05:31,109
Part A asks for the pipeline diagram, the forwarding table and the stall table. It is worth 18 marks.

42
00:05:31,559 --> 00:05:43,271
Two facts of the class datapath decide many cells. First, MX and WX feed only the ALU inputs. So store data has only the WM path.

43
00:05:43,721 --> 00:05:54,943
Second, there is no forwarding into D. A D in the same cycle as the producer's W reads the old value. Pause the video and try it. Then continue.

44
00:05:55,393 --> 00:06:04,673
Step 1: write the producer of each source register. Number 1 and number 2 read x3 and x4. Nothing older writes them.

45
00:06:05,123 --> 00:06:10,989
Number 3 uses x3 as its base address. The producer is the load, number 2.

46
00:06:11,439 --> 00:06:22,042
Number 4 add also reads x3 from number 2. Orange means a load producer. That is a warning: a load value exists only at the end of M.

47
00:06:22,492 --> 00:06:33,457
Number 5 and number 6 are stores. Each uses x6 two times: as the base address and as the store data. The producer is add number 4.

48
00:06:33,907 --> 00:06:37,491
Number 7 reads x8, and nothing older writes it.

49
00:06:37,941 --> 00:06:43,253
Number 8 B E Q reads x6 from number 4. The branch is taken.

50
00:06:43,703 --> 00:06:50,167
So number 9 and number 10 are on the wrong path. They are flushed, so they are not producers.

51
00:06:50,617 --> 00:07:00,388
Number 11 reads x7. Number 9 also writes x7, but number 9 is flushed. So the producer is the load number 7.

52
00:07:00,838 --> 00:07:06,854
Number 12 and number 13 read only x0. x0 is never a hazard.

53
00:07:07,304 --> 00:07:10,696
Number 14 reads x7 from number 11.

54
00:07:11,146 --> 00:07:22,346
Number 14 also reads x8. Number 12 and number 13 both write x8. Number 13 is later, so the load number 13 is the producer.

55
00:07:22,796 --> 00:07:34,849
Now fill the diagram. In cycles 1 to 3, number 1 store word and number 2 load word read x3 and x4. Nothing older writes them, so there is no hazard.

56
00:07:35,299 --> 00:07:47,289
In cycle 4, number 3 is in D. Its base x3 comes from the load number 2. The X of number 3 would be cycle 5. In cycle 5, the load is in M.

57
00:07:47,739 --> 00:07:55,739
The load value exists only at the end of M. This is load-use, so number 3 stalls: D star in cycle 4.

58
00:07:56,189 --> 00:07:59,005
Number 4 repeats F in cycle 4.

59
00:07:59,455 --> 00:08:09,396
In cycle 6, number 3 is in X and the load is in W. x3 goes by WX into the ALU, for the base address.

60
00:08:09,846 --> 00:08:27,894
In cycle 6, number 4 add is in D, and it also needs x3. The load writes x3 in cycle 6, but D sees only earlier writes. So number 4 reads the old x3. This is the W D case, and number 4 repeats D.

61
00:08:28,344 --> 00:08:31,224
Number 5 repeats F in cycle 6.

62
00:08:31,674 --> 00:08:38,543
In cycle 7, number 4 reads x3 again from the register file. Now it gets the new value.

63
00:08:38,993 --> 00:08:42,407
Number 5 does its last F in cycle 7.

64
00:08:42,857 --> 00:08:55,294
Number 5 store word uses x6 as base and as data. In cycle 9, number 5 is in X and add number 4 is in M. The base x6 goes by MX.

65
00:08:55,744 --> 00:09:01,056
Number 6 is in D in cycle 9, and it waits. The next steps show why.

66
00:09:01,506 --> 00:09:11,213
In cycle 10, number 5 is in M and number 4 is in W. The store data x6 of number 5 goes by WM.

67
00:09:11,663 --> 00:09:22,479
Now number 6, seen from cycle 9. It is also a store, with x6 as base and data. Its X would be cycle 10, while number 4 is in W.

68
00:09:22,929 --> 00:09:35,046
The base could use WX. But store data has no WX path, and WM would need number 6 in M in cycle 10. So number 6 stalls in cycle 9.

69
00:09:35,496 --> 00:09:45,224
In cycle 10, number 6 is in D in the cycle of number 4's W. That is the W D case, so number 6 repeats D again.

70
00:09:45,674 --> 00:09:49,109
Number 7 waits in F in cycles 9 and 10.

71
00:09:49,559 --> 00:09:54,017
In cycle 11, number 6 reads x6 from the register file.

72
00:09:54,467 --> 00:09:58,499
Count the D star cells of number 6: 2 stall cycles.

73
00:09:58,949 --> 00:10:02,448
Number 7 does its last F in cycle 11.

74
00:10:02,898 --> 00:10:10,215
In cycle 12, number 7 load word is in D. It reads x8, and nothing older writes x8.

75
00:10:10,665 --> 00:10:22,932
In cycle 13, number 8 B E Q is in D. It reads x6 and x8. Number 4 wrote x6 in cycle 10, long before. So there is no hazard.

76
00:10:23,382 --> 00:10:28,118
In cycle 14, B E Q is in X. It resolves: taken.

77
00:10:28,568 --> 00:10:35,139
The pipeline guessed not taken, so it fetched number 9 and number 10. Both are on the wrong path.

78
00:10:35,589 --> 00:10:42,117
In cycle 15, number 9 and number 10 are flushed. They get a dash in each later cycle.

79
00:10:42,567 --> 00:10:48,903
The target T1, instruction 11, is fetched in cycle 15, the cycle after X.

80
00:10:49,353 --> 00:10:53,577
So the taken branch costs 2 cycles: the 2 flushed rows.

81
00:10:54,027 --> 00:11:10,816
Number 11 needs x7. Number 9 also writes x7, but number 9 is flushed. So the producer is the load number 7. It wrote x7 in cycle 15. Number 11 reads it in D in cycle 16. No stall.

82
00:11:11,266 --> 00:11:19,821
In cycles 17 and 18, number 12 and number 13 read only x0. x0 is never a hazard.

83
00:11:20,271 --> 00:11:33,284
Number 14 add needs x7 and x8. x8 comes from the load number 13. The X of number 14 would be cycle 20, when the load is in M. That is load-use.

84
00:11:33,734 --> 00:11:45,062
x7 comes from number 11. Number 11 is in W in cycle 19, the same cycle as the D of number 14. That is the W D case.

85
00:11:45,512 --> 00:11:51,101
Both reasons ask for a stall in cycle 19. One D star covers both.

86
00:11:51,551 --> 00:11:56,458
In cycle 20, number 14 reads x7 from the register file.

87
00:11:56,908 --> 00:12:05,847
In cycle 21, number 14 is in X and the load number 13 is in W. x8 goes by WX.

88
00:12:06,297 --> 00:12:12,057
Number 14 finishes in cycle 23. So the run takes 23 cycles.

89
00:12:12,507 --> 00:12:15,856
Count the forwarding arrows. There are 4 forwards.

90
00:12:16,306 --> 00:12:20,466
Count the D star cells. There are 5 stall cycles in D.

91
00:12:20,916 --> 00:12:37,129
Now the forwarding table. Each forward is one row. Row 1: source number 2, destination number 3, register x3, from W to X, in cycle 6. The small diagram shows the two instructions in that cycle.

92
00:12:37,579 --> 00:12:46,646
Row 2: from number 4 to number 5, x6, from M to X, in cycle 9. This is the base address of number 5.

93
00:12:47,096 --> 00:12:56,440
Row 3: from number 4 to number 5 again, x6, from W to M, in cycle 10. This is the store data of number 5.

94
00:12:56,890 --> 00:13:05,381
Rows 2 and 3 have the same destination, number 5. One instruction can receive two forwards, one for each source.

95
00:13:05,831 --> 00:13:12,935
Row 4: from number 13 to number 14, x8, from W to X, in cycle 21.

96
00:13:13,385 --> 00:13:26,227
There is no forward for x7 into number 14. Number 11 writes x7 in cycle 19, and number 14 reads it from the register file in D, in cycle 20.

97
00:13:26,677 --> 00:13:38,112
Now the stall table. Each stall gets one row with its stage, its cycles and the reason. Number 3 stalls in D in cycle 4: load-use on x3.

98
00:13:38,562 --> 00:13:43,490
Because number 3 holds D, number 4 stalls in F in cycle 4.

99
00:13:43,940 --> 00:13:57,060
Number 4 stalls in D in cycle 6. The reason is the W D case. Number 2 writes x3 in W in cycle 6. D in the same cycle reads the old value.

100
00:13:57,510 --> 00:14:00,497
Number 5 stalls in F in cycle 6.

101
00:14:00,947 --> 00:14:15,816
Number 6 stalls in D in cycles 9 and 10. Its store data x6 has only the WM path, and WM is not possible in time. So number 6 reads x6 in D after cycle 10.

102
00:14:16,266 --> 00:14:19,679
Number 7 stalls in F in cycles 9 and 10.

103
00:14:20,129 --> 00:14:33,676
Number 14 stalls in D in cycle 19. There are two reasons: load-use on x8 from number 13, and the W D case on x7 from number 11. Write both reasons.

104
00:14:34,126 --> 00:14:42,233
The flushed instructions number 9 and number 10 do not go in the stall table. They go in the diagram, with dashes.

105
00:14:42,683 --> 00:14:50,469
Part B of the same question asks for an optimization of the forwarding hardware, and for the new forwarding logic.

106
00:14:50,919 --> 00:14:54,077
Forwarding into the decode stage is not allowed.

107
00:14:54,527 --> 00:15:02,825
The diagram is the Part A answer: 23 cycles. Pause the video and look for the stalls that a new path could remove.

108
00:15:03,275 --> 00:15:08,331
Number 6 store word stalls 2 cycles in D, in cycles 9 and 10.

109
00:15:08,781 --> 00:15:12,280
Its store data x6 comes from add number 4.

110
00:15:12,730 --> 00:15:18,661
In cycle 10, add number 4 is in W. So the new x6 already exists.

111
00:15:19,111 --> 00:15:25,041
Number 6 could be in X in cycle 10. The base address can already take WX.

112
00:15:25,491 --> 00:15:31,678
Only a path into the store-data input is missing. That missing path is the optimization.

113
00:15:32,128 --> 00:15:43,008
The sketch shows the class pipeline, as on slide 39. The store data, rs2 X, goes from the D X register straight to the X M register.

114
00:15:43,458 --> 00:15:49,879
Now the forwarding paths. MX and WX go into the two muxes in front of the ALU.

115
00:15:50,329 --> 00:15:54,916
WM goes into a mux at the data input of the data memory.

116
00:15:55,366 --> 00:16:00,209
The red line is the store data in X. It passes no forwarding mux.

117
00:16:00,659 --> 00:16:07,613
In cycle 10, add number 4 is in W. Its new x6 is in the M W register.

118
00:16:08,063 --> 00:16:15,978
Number 6 is in D and wants to go to X. No path can bring the new x6 to the store data of number 6.

119
00:16:16,428 --> 00:16:22,636
The optimization: add a mux on the store-data line in X, before the X M register.

120
00:16:23,086 --> 00:16:33,859
Its new input comes from the WX path: the value in the M W register. So the new x6 replaces the old x6 from rs2 X.

121
00:16:34,309 --> 00:16:41,392
The forwarding control logic drives the select input of the new mux. The next slide shows the logic.

122
00:16:41,842 --> 00:16:54,578
The new logic compares register numbers in the pipeline registers, like the existing forwarding logic. Condition 1: the instruction in X is a store. Number 6 is a store.

123
00:16:55,028 --> 00:17:04,329
Condition 2: its rs2 equals the destination register of the instruction in M W. x6 equals x6.

124
00:17:04,779 --> 00:17:10,369
Condition 3: that register is not x0. x6 is not x0.

125
00:17:10,819 --> 00:17:15,277
All three conditions are true, so the AND gives select equal to 1.

126
00:17:15,727 --> 00:17:22,063
With select 1, the mux passes the M W value, the new x6, to rs2 M.

127
00:17:22,513 --> 00:17:26,609
With select 0, it passes rs2 X, as before.

128
00:17:27,059 --> 00:17:39,390
The new logic has the same form as the logic on slide 39. There, if rs1 in D X equals rd in X M, the A mux selects the MX input.

129
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:46,859
If rs1 in D X equals rd in M W, the A mux selects the WX input.

130
00:17:47,309 --> 00:17:57,954
So the answer for Part B: add a WX path into the store-data input, with the select logic above. It is a model answer, not an official one.

131
00:17:58,404 --> 00:18:05,380
The diagram now has the new path. In cycle 9, number 6 is in D, and nothing stops it now.

132
00:18:05,830 --> 00:18:14,150
In cycle 10, add number 4 is in W. WM gives x6 to the store data of number 5, as before.

133
00:18:14,600 --> 00:18:20,211
The new WX path gives x6 to number 6, for its base and its store data.

134
00:18:20,661 --> 00:18:33,098
The run now ends in cycle 21. Part A ended in cycle 23. 23 minus 21 is 2 cycles: the 2 D star cycles of number 6 are gone.

135
00:18:33,548 --> 00:18:39,863
Three stalls are left. Count the D star cells: number 3, number 4 and number 14.

136
00:18:40,313 --> 00:18:49,230
Number 3 and number 14 have load-use stalls. No path can help them, because a load value exists only at the end of M.

137
00:18:49,680 --> 00:18:56,997
Number 4 has the W D case. Only forwarding into D could remove it, and the question excludes that.

138
00:18:57,447 --> 00:19:11,314
Question 2, the answers. Part A ends in cycle 23. Check the count: 4 cycles to fill the pipeline, 12 finished instructions, 5 D star cycles, and 2 flushed cycles.

139
00:19:11,764 --> 00:19:20,724
Write the diagram, the 4 forwards and the 4 stalls in D. Each stall in D also makes the next instruction repeat F.

140
00:19:21,174 --> 00:19:29,622
Part B removes the 2 D star cycles of number 6. 4 plus 12 plus 3 plus 2 is 21 cycles.

141
00:19:30,072 --> 00:19:36,899
The answer for Part B: add a WX path into the store-data input, with its select logic.

142
00:19:37,349 --> 00:19:53,519
Question 3, caches, 12 marks. A 32 kilobyte, 4-way cache with 16 byte blocks. Part A asks for the bits. Part B asks for 5 addresses in the same set but in different ways. Pause the video and try it.

143
00:19:53,969 --> 00:19:59,751
Part A. A block has 16 bytes, which is 2 to the 4. So the offset is 4 bits.

144
00:20:00,201 --> 00:20:05,705
32 kilobytes divided by 16 bytes is 2048 blocks.

145
00:20:06,155 --> 00:20:16,928
2048 blocks divided by 4 ways is 512 sets. 512 is 2 to the 9, so the index is 9 bits.

146
00:20:17,378 --> 00:20:28,962
The paper does not give the address width. Assume 32 bits, as in RISC-V, and say so. Then the tag is 32 minus 9 minus 4, which is 19 bits.

147
00:20:29,412 --> 00:20:34,127
Part B. Same set means the same index bits. Use set 0.

148
00:20:34,577 --> 00:20:41,915
Different ways means different tags. So keep the index and the offset at 0, and change only the tag.

149
00:20:42,365 --> 00:20:54,973
The tag starts at bit 13. So tag plus 1 adds 2 to the 13 to the address. That is 512 sets times 16 bytes: hex 2 0 0 0.

150
00:20:55,423 --> 00:21:07,818
So A to E are hex 0, 2 0 0 0, 4 0 0 0, 6 0 0 0 and 8 0 0 0. Their tags are 0 to 4.

151
00:21:08,268 --> 00:21:23,180
Part C. The table shows 15 accesses to one set. They go to ways 0, 1, 2, 3, 0, 1, and so on, and every access misses. Fill in the labels A to E. Pause the video and try it.

152
00:21:23,630 --> 00:21:28,302
The first four accesses fill the empty ways: A, B, C and D.

153
00:21:28,752 --> 00:21:39,355
Access 5 must miss and go to way 0. So it is a new block, E. LRU replaces A, the least recently used block, in way 0.

154
00:21:39,805 --> 00:21:49,597
Access 6 must miss and go to way 1. A was just evicted, so access A again. It misses, and LRU replaces B in way 1.

155
00:21:50,047 --> 00:21:59,476
The pattern continues: B, C, D, E, A, B, C, D, E. Each access asks for the block that LRU evicted last.

156
00:21:59,926 --> 00:22:05,857
The rule: 5 blocks that cycle through one 4-way L R U set miss every time.

157
00:22:06,307 --> 00:22:16,440
Question 3, the answers. Part A: offset 4 bits, index 9 bits, and tag 19 bits. Write that you assume 32-bit addresses.

158
00:22:16,890 --> 00:22:29,477
Part B: hex 0, 2 0 0 0, 4 0 0 0, 6 0 0 0 and 8 0 0 0. Same index, tags 0 to 4.

159
00:22:29,927 --> 00:22:37,692
Part C: A B C D E, three times. Each access asks for the block that L R U evicted last.

160
00:22:38,142 --> 00:22:52,051
A design question asks you to change the pipeline. Use the same six questions every time. First, the new state. Which new registers hold the new values? In which stage are they read and written?

161
00:22:52,501 --> 00:22:58,560
Second, the paths and the muxes. Which wires carry the new values? Which mux selects them?

162
00:22:59,010 --> 00:23:04,578
Third, the control signals. The decoder sets new signals for each new instruction.

163
00:23:05,028 --> 00:23:13,647
Fourth, the hazards. Find the new read after write cases. Then decide: forward by MX or WX, or stall.

164
00:23:14,097 --> 00:23:19,302
Fifth, precise state. Write new state in W, after M, the commit point.

165
00:23:19,752 --> 00:23:25,747
Sixth, run a program on the new hardware. Fill the pipeline diagram and count the cycles.

166
00:23:26,197 --> 00:23:33,535
A design question gives marks for each of these six parts. So name every new register, mux and signal.

167
00:23:33,985 --> 00:23:46,529
Question 4 of the fall 2019 midterm. Homer adds two 32-bit registers, HI and LO, in the decode stage. He also adds three new instructions.

168
00:23:46,979 --> 00:23:52,505
W mul multiplies register rs1 by register rs2.

169
00:23:52,955 --> 00:24:00,272
Each register has 32 bits. So the product has 32 plus 32, which is 64 bits.

170
00:24:00,722 --> 00:24:05,543
W mul writes the upper 32 bits of the product into HI.

171
00:24:05,993 --> 00:24:08,937
It writes the lower 32 bits into LO.

172
00:24:09,387 --> 00:24:13,675
Move from HI copies HI into register rd.

173
00:24:14,125 --> 00:24:17,944
Move from LO copies LO into register rd.

174
00:24:18,394 --> 00:24:25,413
The question asks for all the necessary changes to the 5-stage pipeline. Pause the video and try it.

175
00:24:25,863 --> 00:24:34,439
Start with one row for each instruction. W mul reads rs1 and rs2 in D, from the register file.

176
00:24:34,889 --> 00:24:38,729
In X, a multiplier makes the 64-bit product.

177
00:24:39,179 --> 00:24:46,837
We assume that the multiplier takes one X cycle. The question gives no latency, so state this in the exam.

178
00:24:47,287 --> 00:24:57,847
In M, the X M register carries the product. A class pipeline register holds a 32-bit result. So X M needs 32 more bits.

179
00:24:58,297 --> 00:25:09,412
In W, W mul writes HI and LO. The M W register also carries all 64 bits, so it also gets 32 more bits.

180
00:25:09,862 --> 00:25:17,094
Move from HI and move from LO read HI or LO in D. A new mux selects the value.

181
00:25:17,544 --> 00:25:21,000
In X, the ALU passes the value to its output.

182
00:25:21,450 --> 00:25:28,853
In W, the instruction writes rd, as any ALU instruction does. Reg W En is 1.

183
00:25:29,303 --> 00:25:36,044
So treat HI and LO like the register file. Read them in D, and write them in W.

184
00:25:36,494 --> 00:25:50,190
Do not write HI and LO at the end of X. An older instruction can still raise an exception in M. The handler then sees a result of a younger instruction. That state is not precise.

185
00:25:50,640 --> 00:25:56,443
W comes after M, the commit point. So a write in W keeps the state precise.

186
00:25:56,893 --> 00:26:05,170
The figure shows the class pipeline with forwarding, in gray. It has the five stages F, D, X, M and W.

187
00:26:05,620 --> 00:26:13,044
The green line is the MX path. It carries a result from the X M register to the ALU input A.

188
00:26:13,494 --> 00:26:20,171
The blue line is the WX path. It carries a result from W to the ALU input A.

189
00:26:20,621 --> 00:26:24,845
Change one. Add the two new registers, HI and LO, in D.

190
00:26:25,295 --> 00:26:36,922
Change two. Add a mux in D, before the D X register. Its select signal, H L Sel, picks rs1, HI or LO for the ALU input A.

191
00:26:37,372 --> 00:26:43,644
Change three. Extend the ALU with a multiplier. Its result has 64 bits.

192
00:26:44,094 --> 00:26:54,675
A class pipeline register holds a 32-bit result. So the X M and M W registers each grow from 32 to 64 bits.

193
00:26:55,125 --> 00:27:01,995
Change four. In W, bits 63 to 32 of the M W register go to HI.

194
00:27:02,445 --> 00:27:04,941
Bits 31 to 0 go to LO.

195
00:27:05,391 --> 00:27:12,303
A new write enable, H L W En, controls this write. It is 1 only for W mul.

196
00:27:12,753 --> 00:27:19,153
Change five. The forwarding mux of input A must select the correct half of a forwarded product.

197
00:27:19,603 --> 00:27:22,056
Move from LO takes the lower half.

198
00:27:22,506 --> 00:27:25,130
Move from HI takes the upper half.

199
00:27:25,580 --> 00:27:34,433
Now the decoder. W mul sets H L Sel to rs1. So the ALU input A gets the rs1 value.

200
00:27:34,883 --> 00:27:37,187
A L U Sel selects multiply.

201
00:27:37,637 --> 00:27:42,288
H L W En is 1, so W writes HI and LO.

202
00:27:42,738 --> 00:27:47,943
Reg W En is 0, because W mul writes no x register.

203
00:27:48,393 --> 00:27:51,743
Move from HI sets H L Sel to HI.

204
00:27:52,193 --> 00:27:57,142
A L U Sel is pass A. The ALU passes input A to its output.

205
00:27:57,592 --> 00:28:01,304
Reg W En is 1, so W writes rd.

206
00:28:01,754 --> 00:28:04,783
W B Sel selects the ALU result.

207
00:28:05,233 --> 00:28:09,543
Move from LO is the same, but H L Sel selects LO.

208
00:28:09,993 --> 00:28:13,726
All other instructions set H L Sel to rs1.

209
00:28:14,176 --> 00:28:19,488
They set H L W En to 0, so they never write HI or LO.

210
00:28:19,938 --> 00:28:21,986
Their other signals do not change.

211
00:28:22,436 --> 00:28:28,644
The names H L Sel and H L W En are our names. The question gives no names.

212
00:28:29,094 --> 00:28:35,558
Next, the hazards. Only W mul writes HI and LO, and it writes them in W.

213
00:28:36,008 --> 00:28:43,091
Move from HI and move from LO read HI or LO in D. They use the value in X.

214
00:28:43,541 --> 00:28:49,151
So the forwarding unit compares opcodes. It does not need to compare register numbers.

215
00:28:49,601 --> 00:28:56,279
Case one, MX. In this short run, move from HI, number 2, is in X in cycle 4.

216
00:28:56,729 --> 00:29:03,982
W mul, number 1, is in M in the same cycle. The X M register holds the new HI.

217
00:29:04,432 --> 00:29:09,211
So mux A selects the X M register. This is the MX condition.

218
00:29:09,661 --> 00:29:16,146
Case two, WX. In this run, move from LO, number 3, is in X in cycle 5.

219
00:29:16,596 --> 00:29:19,903
W mul is in W in the same cycle.

220
00:29:20,353 --> 00:29:28,118
The instruction in M, number 2, is not a W mul. A W mul in M would hold a newer value.

221
00:29:28,568 --> 00:29:34,115
So mux A selects the M W register. This is the WX condition.

222
00:29:34,565 --> 00:29:40,325
Case three. In this run, move from HI, number 4, is in D in cycle 5.

223
00:29:40,775 --> 00:29:48,711
W mul is in W in the same cycle. There is no forwarding into D, so D reads the old HI.

224
00:29:49,161 --> 00:29:54,494
So number 4 stalls one cycle: D star. This is the W D case.

225
00:29:54,944 --> 00:30:07,765
The x registers need no change. W mul reads rs1 and rs2, and the move instructions write rd. The class forwarding and stall logic handles these.

226
00:30:08,215 --> 00:30:15,789
Part B asks you to run this sequence on the new pipeline. Use arrows for forwarding. Pause the video and try it.

227
00:30:16,239 --> 00:30:26,201
Number 1, W mul, multiplies in X in cycle 3. We assume one X cycle, so the product exists at the end of cycle 3.

228
00:30:26,651 --> 00:30:30,534
Number 2, move from LO, is in X in cycle 4.

229
00:30:30,984 --> 00:30:38,003
W mul is in M in cycle 4. So LO goes by MX, from the X M register.

230
00:30:38,453 --> 00:30:42,591
Number 3, move from HI, is in X in cycle 5.

231
00:30:43,041 --> 00:30:51,041
W mul is in W in cycle 5. So HI goes by WX, from the M W register.

232
00:30:51,491 --> 00:30:55,374
Number 4, move from LO, is in D in cycle 5.

233
00:30:55,824 --> 00:31:07,216
W mul writes LO in W in the same cycle. There is no forwarding into D. So D reads the old LO, and number 4 repeats D: D star.

234
00:31:07,666 --> 00:31:12,317
Number 4 holds D, so number 5 repeats F: F star.

235
00:31:12,767 --> 00:31:16,543
In cycle 6, number 4 reads the new LO in D.

236
00:31:16,993 --> 00:31:21,131
In cycle 7, the store, number 5, reads x 6 in D.

237
00:31:21,581 --> 00:31:27,853
Number 2 wrote x 6 in W in cycle 6. So the register file already has it.

238
00:31:28,303 --> 00:31:30,479
The sequence takes 10 cycles.

239
00:31:30,929 --> 00:31:35,687
There is one stall cycle in D and one in F, both in cycle 5.

240
00:31:36,137 --> 00:31:43,283
Forwarding table, row 1. Number 1 forwards LO to number 2, from M to X, in cycle 4.

241
00:31:43,733 --> 00:31:50,133
Row 2. Number 1 forwards HI to number 3, from W to X, in cycle 5.

242
00:31:50,583 --> 00:32:02,103
Stall table, row 1. Number 4 stalls in D in cycle 5. W mul writes LO in W in the same cycle, so D reads the old LO.

243
00:32:02,553 --> 00:32:08,569
Row 2. Number 5 stalls in F in cycle 5, because number 4 holds D.

244
00:32:09,019 --> 00:32:19,771
This answer uses the model design of Part A, with a one-cycle multiply. With a slower multiplier, the cycles change. So state your assumption.

245
00:32:20,221 --> 00:32:29,139
Part C. Homer removes the branch compare unit from X. The ALU compares instead, and a new adder computes the target.

246
00:32:29,589 --> 00:32:37,951
The figure shows the X stage of the class pipeline. There, the ALU computes the branch target, PC plus immediate.

247
00:32:38,401 --> 00:32:44,503
The branch compare unit takes its inputs from the D X register, before the forwarding muxes.

248
00:32:44,953 --> 00:32:53,699
So the compare unit gets no forwarded values. The branch must read its operands in D, after the W of the producer.

249
00:32:54,149 --> 00:33:01,765
In Homer's design, the ALU compares the two operands. It gives B R equal and B R less than.

250
00:33:02,215 --> 00:33:11,495
The ALU inputs come after the forwarding muxes. So the branch gets its operands by MX or WX, with no new paths.

251
00:33:11,945 --> 00:33:19,967
The ALU is now busy with the compare. So a new adder computes the target, PC plus immediate, at the same time.

252
00:33:20,417 --> 00:33:22,571
The branch still resolves in X.

253
00:33:23,021 --> 00:33:27,715
Pause the video and try it. Find how many stall cycles Homer saves.

254
00:33:28,165 --> 00:33:34,287
First, the class pipeline. The branch, number 2, comes right after add, which writes x 1.

255
00:33:34,737 --> 00:33:38,151
Add writes x 1 in W, in cycle 5.

256
00:33:38,601 --> 00:33:44,979
The branch must read x 1 in D after that W. So it reads x 1 in cycle 6.

257
00:33:45,429 --> 00:33:51,808
Before that, the branch repeats D in cycles 3, 4 and 5. That is 3 stall cycles.

258
00:33:52,258 --> 00:33:56,290
In Homer's design, the branch is in X in cycle 4.

259
00:33:56,740 --> 00:34:02,756
Add is in M in cycle 4. So x 1 goes by MX, and there is no stall.

260
00:34:03,206 --> 00:34:06,043
The run takes 8 cycles, not 11.

261
00:34:06,493 --> 00:34:11,571
With a load as the producer, the class pipeline also stalls 3 cycles.

262
00:34:12,021 --> 00:34:15,711
Homer's design stalls one cycle: the load-use case.

263
00:34:16,161 --> 00:34:19,788
Then x 1 goes by WX, in cycle 5.

264
00:34:20,238 --> 00:34:23,161
The run takes 9 cycles, not 11.

265
00:34:23,611 --> 00:34:29,115
Now a taken branch in Homer's design. It resolves in X, in cycle 4.

266
00:34:29,565 --> 00:34:33,362
The two younger instructions are flushed. They show dashes.

267
00:34:33,812 --> 00:34:42,559
The target is fetched in cycle 5, not 3. So a wrong guess still costs 2 cycles: the fetches in cycles 3 and 4.

268
00:34:43,009 --> 00:34:52,630
The answer for Part C: Homer removes the stalls of a branch that uses a recent result. The penalty of a wrong guess does not change.

269
00:34:53,080 --> 00:35:04,109
Question 4, the answers. Part A: name each new register, mux, signal and hazard case. HI and LO are read in D and written in W.

270
00:35:04,559 --> 00:35:17,317
Part B: the run takes 10 cycles. LO goes by MX, HI goes by WX, and number 4 stalls 1 cycle in D. State your one-cycle multiply.

271
00:35:17,767 --> 00:35:30,033
Part C: the branch gets its operands through the forwarding muxes of the ALU. This removes the stalls after a recent result. A wrong guess still costs 2 cycles.

272
00:35:30,483 --> 00:35:42,729
Recap. Question 1: Amdahl's law is 1 divided by the sum of 1 minus f and f over s. For a RAW pair, use the most recent older writer.

273
00:35:43,179 --> 00:35:56,981
Question 2: find the producers first. Then test each source register: MX, WX, WM, or a stall. Store data has only WM, and there is no forwarding into D.

274
00:35:57,431 --> 00:36:09,442
Question 3: offset bits from the block size, index bits from the number of sets, and the tag is the rest. 5 blocks in a 4-way L R U set miss every time.

275
00:36:09,892 --> 00:36:16,100
Question 4: use the six design questions, from the new state to a run of the program.

276
00:36:16,550 --> 00:36:29,755
Before you hand in a diagram, check the count: 4, plus the finished instructions, plus the D star cycles, plus 2 for each taken branch. This check is our own; it is not on the slides.
